WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.790
- Next up, coming in from Luigi,

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.240
"Any suggestions on how
to better communicate

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.373
"with people from other countries?

00:00:10.373 --> 00:00:11.635
"I mean foreign languages,

00:00:11.635 --> 00:00:13.913
"different cultures, different beliefs."

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:16.732
So, I mean, Steve is a
great guy to talk to.

00:00:16.732 --> 00:00:18.732
He's got offices around the world,

00:00:18.732 --> 00:00:21.274
thousands and thousands of employees.

00:00:21.274 --> 00:00:23.714
How do you handle it, Stephen?

00:00:23.714 --> 00:00:27.812
- Yeah, I think Luigi, I
think the great thing is,

00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:30.107
and this is the power of the Internet,

00:00:30.107 --> 00:00:32.747
is everything's available.

00:00:32.747 --> 00:00:34.581
'Cause I think there's
a love about business,

00:00:34.581 --> 00:00:37.138
that you want to become,
always educating yourself

00:00:37.138 --> 00:00:41.305
more and more, and you
know, Evan, I speak to you,

00:00:42.661 --> 00:00:45.410
great business people are
just great people in life.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.864
Normally they're very
tolerant, they love diversity,

00:00:47.864 --> 00:00:52.031
they love inclusion, they've
got tremendous outreach,

00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:57.302
and they kind of love the
stranger, in many respects.

00:00:57.302 --> 00:00:58.541
And when you're going and you look at

00:00:58.541 --> 00:01:00.302
your business, Luigi, whether you're going

00:01:00.302 --> 00:01:02.240
kind of across the
border or you're looking

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.181
to going out to Asia or
Europe, first of all,

00:01:05.181 --> 00:01:08.769
I'd almost say again, back
to kind of Evan said it is,

00:01:08.769 --> 00:01:12.936
do a bit of research about
the culture, the difference,

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:16.061
and how your idea might translate.

00:01:16.061 --> 00:01:18.974
Then the other thing is, the
great thing about if you live

00:01:18.974 --> 00:01:22.000
in Canada or US, you'll
find loads of people,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.833
whether they come from,
could be Asia, Africa.

00:01:26.839 --> 00:01:30.384
They're probably within half
a mile of where you are.

00:01:30.384 --> 00:01:32.105
Go and test-drive some stuff in terms of

00:01:32.105 --> 00:01:35.053
how it might work with them.

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:38.220
The great thing I've
experienced is obviously,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.600
the world is diverse, we
celebrate the tolerance,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.081
and actually everything
is fantastic about,

00:01:45.081 --> 00:01:47.686
cities like Toronto, you
walk down the street there,

00:01:47.686 --> 00:01:49.915
it's a fantastic multicultural center.

00:01:49.915 --> 00:01:52.052
Same with New York, same with London,

00:01:52.052 --> 00:01:54.559
but in reality, what I would say,

00:01:54.559 --> 00:01:56.491
the nice thing about
products and services,

00:01:56.491 --> 00:02:00.160
if you got a great idea, it can go global.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.587
It can go viral, and the
Internet has no respect

00:02:04.587 --> 00:02:07.702
for international boundaries,
and that's the game changer

00:02:07.702 --> 00:02:09.535
in the last 13 years.

00:02:09.535 --> 00:02:11.301
That gives you a huge platform

00:02:11.301 --> 00:02:14.818
cuz you now have a
canvas that is the world.

00:02:14.818 --> 00:02:17.965
That is your canvas,
that is your playground.

00:02:17.965 --> 00:02:20.735
So I definitely think
it's worth going for it,

00:02:20.735 --> 00:02:22.436
look into it and do research on the web,

00:02:22.436 --> 00:02:24.603
and then, road-test stuff.

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:30.076
Launch a (bad audio) an Italian website.

00:02:31.146 --> 00:02:32.881
Before you had to get translated stuff.

00:02:32.881 --> 00:02:35.332
You can do (bad audio) translation stuff,

00:02:35.332 --> 00:02:37.818
then get an Italian friend
just to cast their eye over it,

00:02:37.818 --> 00:02:38.910
make sure it all makes sense.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.823
You can go and have an e-commerce
platform with your product

00:02:41.823 --> 00:02:44.139
and test-drive it into that marketplace.

00:02:44.139 --> 00:02:47.666
It's just incredible, we've
got loads of customers,

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:50.584
great customer of ours, Jen
O'Price, who started a business

00:02:50.584 --> 00:02:53.750
up in the north of England, Yorkshire.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:57.653
She started a great organic,
really healthy food business,

00:02:57.653 --> 00:03:01.144
and now, I think she
exports to 29 countries,

00:03:01.144 --> 00:03:03.440
loads of different currencies,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.205
and when she started her journey,

00:03:05.205 --> 00:03:09.288
she was passionate about
healthy, organic eating.

00:03:10.688 --> 00:03:13.405
That was her dream, and really,

00:03:13.405 --> 00:03:16.895
if we're honest with ourself,
she probably didn't know

00:03:16.895 --> 00:03:18.697
she'd be as successful as she has been,

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:21.199
and she didn't know she'd
be in as many countries.

00:03:21.199 --> 00:03:23.228
But I think what the power is, and again,

00:03:23.228 --> 00:03:25.868
it comes back to kind of
believing in yourself,

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:29.421
and everything Evan talks
to, is if you've got

00:03:29.421 --> 00:03:32.102
a great product, almost,
if you put it out there,

00:03:32.102 --> 00:03:34.668
the market will find it,
it will create a pull,

00:03:34.668 --> 00:03:36.407
instead of what was the old way

00:03:36.407 --> 00:03:38.945
where you had to push, push, push, push.

00:03:38.945 --> 00:03:42.482
So you've got that kind of
canvas around the world,

00:03:42.482 --> 00:03:45.139
find out and do the research,
and then I would go for it.

00:03:45.139 --> 00:03:49.413
Road-test it, test-drive it,
and scale up on what works.

00:03:49.413 --> 00:03:50.783
Evan?

00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:52.028
- Yeah, I have two thoughts.

00:03:52.028 --> 00:03:54.121
The first thing that comes to mind

00:03:54.121 --> 00:03:56.801
is that the intent is most important.

00:03:56.801 --> 00:04:00.645
I think a lot of people avoid
going into another market

00:04:00.645 --> 00:04:02.119
or dealing with people
from different cultures

00:04:02.119 --> 00:04:04.027
because they're just really
worried that they don't

00:04:04.027 --> 00:04:06.081
speak the language or they
don't know the culture,

00:04:06.081 --> 00:04:08.618
and that prevents them
from taking any action.

00:04:08.618 --> 00:04:10.294
Your intent is most important.

00:04:10.294 --> 00:04:13.821
If you know that you can
solve this person's problem

00:04:13.821 --> 00:04:16.617
in that market, then you
should go out and do it,

00:04:16.617 --> 00:04:18.271
because it's coming from a good place.

00:04:18.271 --> 00:04:19.830
Yes, you're going to make some mistakes,

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:21.715
you're going to trip up a couple times,

00:04:21.715 --> 00:04:23.986
and the thing that gets
you through is the intent.

00:04:23.986 --> 00:04:26.751
You're not going in there
trying to harm them,

00:04:26.751 --> 00:04:28.511
you're going in there trying to help,

00:04:28.511 --> 00:04:30.253
and even if you make a couple mistakes,

00:04:30.253 --> 00:04:33.329
you say the wrong word that
maybe, you mispronounce it,

00:04:33.329 --> 00:04:34.819
it becomes something offensive,

00:04:34.819 --> 00:04:37.050
you can work your way through
it because they can feel

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.416
the genuine care that
you have, to try to help.

00:04:40.416 --> 00:04:43.228
Don't let that fear of
not knowing the culture

00:04:43.228 --> 00:04:45.676
prevent you from doing something
potentially really helpful

00:04:45.676 --> 00:04:48.094
and great in that market.

00:04:48.094 --> 00:04:49.427
So that's first-

00:04:50.316 --> 00:04:51.319
- You've got your second point,

00:04:51.319 --> 00:04:53.291
you go for your second point.

00:04:53.291 --> 00:04:56.125
No, go on, I'm just in the
mood now, so go for it.

00:04:56.125 --> 00:04:58.540
- Okay, the second point was I
think, something that Stephen

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.109
does actually really well,
is he's always hyper-aware

00:05:02.109 --> 00:05:04.425
of what's happening in
the markets of the places

00:05:04.425 --> 00:05:07.087
that he's going to, and
so every time we get on,

00:05:07.087 --> 00:05:08.833
he's talking about the Blue Jays,

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:11.361
he's talking about my prime minister,

00:05:11.361 --> 00:05:13.346
he's always super aware
of what's happening

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:14.840
in the markets he's going to.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.947
I would like to think that
Stephen's favorite baseball team

00:05:17.947 --> 00:05:19.690
of all time is the Toronto Blue Jays.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.617
You're going to break my
heart Stephen, if you say no.

00:05:21.617 --> 00:05:22.867
- Yes, go Jays.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.249
- But he's really great at
that, and so if you know

00:05:26.249 --> 00:05:28.540
you're going to a local
market, just understanding

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:31.089
some things about them,
understanding what's relevant

00:05:31.089 --> 00:05:34.111
in the news, understanding,
I like baseball

00:05:34.111 --> 00:05:35.844
but I don't pay attention to other things.

00:05:35.844 --> 00:05:38.413
It allows you to have some
points of commonality,

00:05:38.413 --> 00:05:40.121
and doing a little bit more
research and groundwork

00:05:40.121 --> 00:05:42.096
before going in can really help,

00:05:42.096 --> 00:05:44.082
just a couple of touchpoints really helps,

00:05:44.082 --> 00:05:48.577
makes a difference when you're
communicating with them.

00:05:48.577 --> 00:05:51.350
- When you're talking there,
and I was just listening,

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.846
and again, you've got
a great gift of putting

00:05:53.846 --> 00:05:55.953
the human touch on things,
just a little thing I do

00:05:55.953 --> 00:05:57.239
when I go around the world,

00:05:57.239 --> 00:05:59.022
I'm in Berlin next week, I'm presenting,

00:05:59.022 --> 00:06:01.419
I don't know, couple of thousand people,

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:05.336
but my first three
sentences will be in German.

00:06:06.291 --> 00:06:09.378
If you're going to be, or
you get on Skype with people

00:06:09.378 --> 00:06:12.345
in I don't know, Italy, down in Brazil,

00:06:12.345 --> 00:06:14.326
and you're looking to strike
some sort of partnership

00:06:14.326 --> 00:06:16.311
or distribution agreement with them,

00:06:16.311 --> 00:06:20.068
it's pretty powerful, especially
actually face-to-face.

00:06:20.068 --> 00:06:23.139
If you learn a couple of opening,

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:27.222
you just say bonjour, je
m'appelle Stephen Kelly.

00:06:29.303 --> 00:06:32.129
I go to Canada, up to Montreal,

00:06:32.129 --> 00:06:35.504
if I can speak a little bit in French ...

00:06:35.504 --> 00:06:38.477
That means you break the ice,
cuz I think what we forget,

00:06:38.477 --> 00:06:41.408
and it's so easy to forget,
because it's kind of voices

00:06:41.408 --> 00:06:44.357
in our head tell us people are strangers,

00:06:44.357 --> 00:06:46.482
but the reality is, when you
show a bit of vulnerability

00:06:46.482 --> 00:06:48.629
like that and say, look, I
don't speak very good French

00:06:48.629 --> 00:06:51.713
but at least I had a go
because I respect you,

00:06:51.713 --> 00:06:53.881
and I respect your nation and culture.

00:06:53.881 --> 00:06:57.552
Then I've always found people reach out

00:06:57.552 --> 00:07:00.428
and actually embrace you,
and it's very powerful.

00:07:00.428 --> 00:07:02.729
It's just a very human thing to say,

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:05.209
but it's something where you can go to,

00:07:05.209 --> 00:07:07.420
and if you looking to go overseas,

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.771
then as you say, do a bit of
research about what's topical,

00:07:09.771 --> 00:07:11.270
what's happening in the news,

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:14.058
what's happening with Justin Trudeau

00:07:14.058 --> 00:07:15.963
and all that sort of stuff
when we go to Canada,

00:07:15.963 --> 00:07:19.152
if the Blue Jays are winning.

00:07:19.152 --> 00:07:21.476
But then also, if you can, say a few words

00:07:21.476 --> 00:07:23.661
in their language, it's so powerful.

00:07:23.661 --> 00:07:25.181
I remember when I used to go to Tokyo,

00:07:25.181 --> 00:07:27.398
I had a business out there, and literally

00:07:27.398 --> 00:07:30.266
first couple of sentences of saying kanji,

00:07:30.266 --> 00:07:34.148
and then that exhausted my
totality of my Japanese,

00:07:34.148 --> 00:07:38.670
but honestly, you hear
stuff about the Japanese.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.221
They're very civilized and
they're very respectful

00:07:41.221 --> 00:07:43.880
and they're lovely people,
but you hear it's really hard

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.210
to go to Japan, it's a
difficult market to break into,

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:50.041
but I think if you show
a bit of interest in them

00:07:50.041 --> 00:07:53.518
and their culture, and if you
can even say two sentences,

00:07:53.518 --> 00:07:56.404
then you find them grabbing
you and bringing you

00:07:56.404 --> 00:08:00.571
into their community and making
a success in their world.

